CopyPasteBin 1.1 Debian package
===============================

This package installs CopyPasteBin as a system GNOME Shell extension. It does
not enable the extension during package installation because package manager
actions run as root and must not modify an arbitrary user's GNOME settings.

After installation, log out and back in to GNOME Wayland. Open the application
menu and choose "Enable CopyPasteBin" (Polish: "Włącz CopyPasteBin"). This
explicit action enables only the CopyPasteBin extension for the currently
logged-in user. It does not enable the extension for other accounts. The
launcher refuses activation while a user-local extension with the same UUID
exists, preventing GNOME from silently selecting older code.

The package contains AppStream metadata, the GPL-3.0-or-later project license,
the developer name, homepage and product icon. A graphical installer may still
describe a locally downloaded DEB as untrusted or from an unknown publisher
because it does not come from a configured, signed package repository. A
native DEB is not sandboxed, so Ubuntu may also describe its permissions as
unrestricted. These system warnings are not hidden or bypassed by the package.

If CopyPasteBin was previously installed from the offline archive, run that
archive's current uninstall.sh before installing this package. The offline
uninstaller preserves ~/.config/copypastebin and ~/.local/state/copypastebin.
A user-local extension with the same UUID takes precedence over this system
extension. CopyPasteBin 1.1 uses `/usr/bin/cpb` first when both backends are
available, but an older user-local copy of the extension does not contain that
resolver and could keep using the old user-local backend. The DEB launcher
therefore requires removal of the old offline program code before activation;
it does not remove that code or private data automatically.

Removing this package removes only package-owned program files. User settings
and metadata history under ~/.config/copypastebin and
~/.local/state/copypastebin are not package-owned and remain in place.
